// ============================================================================================
// F-310 MITIGATION RIG — ARM (e) `two-phase escrow Cell`
// 00006-unbalanced-zswap Plan 05 / gate G5. EXPERIMENTAL_LANE / LANE-DEV-1.
// ============================================================================================
//
// Issue 0004 mitigation 1d in its STRONGER form, the owner's proposal of 2026-08-20 as sharpened in
// that issue: do not try to make the offer circuit's map reads cheaper — REMOVE THEM.
//
// THE ARGUMENT, and why it is directionally supported by the data rather than merely plausible. F-310
// measured a flip from GUARANTEED to FALLIBLE when custody went from ONE cell to TWO, with the circuit
// unchanged. Whatever the exact cost curve, a transcript that reads a handful of CELLS and no MAP AT
// ALL cannot move along it: its cost does not depend on how much custody the Manager holds. So this arm
// is the only one whose payoff could be SIZE-INDEPENDENT rather than merely larger, and that is the
// property the rig is asked to test ("(e) if it achieves size-independence", Plan 05 Phase 3).
//
// THE SHAPE IS THREE CIRCUITS, and the split is load-bearing:
//
//   stageOffer(colour, value)   SELF-BALANCED, not an offer. Moves `value` of `colour` out of the big
//                               custody maps into a dedicated escrow CELL, debiting the maker's cell.
//                               It reads and writes `pools` and `shieldedBalances` exactly as v4's
//                               `withdrawShielded` does, so it is as expensive as any ordinary custody
//                               operation — and that is FINE, because F-310 constrains PUBLISHABILITY,
//                               which is a property of offers. A self-balanced transaction the maker
//                               submits itself needs no segment-0 placement at all. This is the claim
//                               the arm rests on and the rig measures it rather than assuming it.
//
//   openSwap(recipientA, coinB) THE OFFER, and the only circuit whose placement matters. It reads the
//                               escrow CELLS and NOTHING ELSE — no `pools`, no `shieldedBalances` — and
//                               writes only cells. The give leg consumes the staged coin (as a surplus
//                               for the OPEN shape, or paid to a named key for the v1/bearer shape);
//                               the want leg lands `coinB` in a SECOND staging cell.
//
//   consolidate()               SELF-BALANCED, not an offer. Merges the received coin back into
//                               `pools` and credits `shieldedBalances`. Runs at any custody size.
//
// WHY THE RECEIVE SIDE NEEDS ITS OWN CELL — issue 0004's caution (ii), and it is the subtle half.
// Crediting colour B straight back into the big maps INSIDE the offer circuit would re-import exactly
// the cost the staging removed: `pools.member(B)`, possibly a `mergeCoinImmediate`, a
// `shieldedKey` hash and a cell read/write. The offer would be as expensive as v4's again and the arm
// would measure nothing. So the received coin is parked in a cell and consolidated later.
//
// AUTHORIZATION IS NOT DROPPED, and this is a deliberate cost the arm pays. It would be cheaper for
// `openSwap` to touch no map whatsoever — but `accounts` is a map, and `authenticatedAccount()` reads
// it. Removing the witness choke point from the offer circuit would mean ANY caller could publish an
// offer spending somebody else's staged coin, choosing the wanted colour and amount themselves. That
// is theft, not an optimisation. So `openSwap` keeps the choke point (ONE `Set.member` plus one
// commitment hash) and additionally asserts the caller IS the account that staged the coin. NC-305
// therefore still holds for this arm, and the residual map read is recorded as the floor this design
// cannot go below without breaking authorization.
//
// ATOMICITY, stated honestly. The SETTLEMENT is still ONE transaction: `openSwap` is a single call and
// the taker's balancing intent merges into it, exactly as in v4. What is now spread over three
// transactions is the CUSTODY BOOKKEEPING around it. Both extra steps fail benignly:
//   * `stageOffer` succeeded but no offer was ever published => the staged coin is still contract-owned
//     and `escrowOwner` records whose it is. Nothing is lost; a `cancelStage` would return it, and its
//     absence here is a fixture limitation, not a design claim (see R5').
//   * `openSwap` settled but `consolidate` has not run => the received coin is contract-owned and
//     `receivedOwner` records who it belongs to. The maker's `shieldedBalances` cell is stale-low
//     until consolidation, which is visible in custody state and is the price of the split.
//
// RELAXATIONS:
//   R1   the unshielded family, inherited from `v4-slim`.
//   R3'' **THE ESCROW IS A SINGLE GLOBAL SLOT.** One staged offer per Manager at a time, contract-wide.
//        Keying the escrow by account (`Map<account, ...>`) would restore a map traversal to the offer
//        circuit and so partly undo the arm. The single slot is therefore measured as the BEST CASE —
//        the cheapest offer transcript this approach can ever have — and any real product would need
//        the keyed form and would have to be re-measured. Not hidden: quoted as an upper bound.
//   R4'' The give AMOUNT is fixed at staging time: `openSwap` gives the WHOLE staged coin. A maker who
//        wants a different amount re-stages. (Giving part of the staged coin is possible via
//        `sendShielded` change back into the escrow cell, at the cost of one more cell write; the
//        whole-coin form is measured because it is the cheap one.)
//   R5'' No `cancelStage` circuit. A staged coin can only leave the escrow through `openSwap`. This is
//        purely a circuit-budget economy in a measurement fixture (see the budget note below) and it
//        is a REAL GAP for any product: it means a maker who stages and then changes their mind has no
//        way back. Recorded as such rather than as a design position.
//   R6'' `consolidate` credits `receivedOwner`, so the received colour's attribution is DELAYED rather
//        than atomic with the swap. FR-205's per-colour conservation invariant therefore holds only
//        BETWEEN phases, not within them: while `receivedActive` is true the contract holds a coin that
//        no `shieldedBalances` cell accounts for. The offline suite asserts the invariant in the
//        extended form "pool value + escrowed + received == sum of cells + staged", which is the
//        honest restatement.
//
// CIRCUIT BUDGET (F-307). v4 sits at exactly 13 provable circuits, the measured ceiling. This arm is
// `v4-slim`'s 8 provable circuits (the unshielded family dropped, R1) minus `openSwapShielded` plus
// `stageOffer`, `openSwap` and `consolidate` — 11 in total, comfortably under. That is what the
// relaxation bought, and it is the reason R1 exists at all.

import CompactStandardLibrary;

export {
  registerAccount,
  depositShielded,
  withdrawShielded,
  transferInternalShielded,
  stageOffer,
  openSwap,
  consolidate,
  zswapNullifierOf,
  zswapCommitmentOf,
  myAccount,
  isRegistered,
  shieldedAccountBalance,
  poolValue,
  poolHasColour,
  shieldedKey,
  accounts,
  shieldedBalances,
  pools,
  escrowActive,
  escrowCoin,
  escrowOwner,
  receivedActive,
  receivedCoin,
  receivedOwner
}

// --- ordinary custody, exactly v4-slim's ------------------------------------------------------

export ledger pools: Map<Bytes<32>, QualifiedShieldedCoinInfo>;
export ledger accounts: Set<Bytes<32>>;
export ledger shieldedBalances: Map<Bytes<32>, Uint<128>>;

// --- the escrow: SIX CELLS, and the offer circuit reads only these ----------------------------
//
// Every one of these is a `Cell`, which is the point: a cell read is a fixed number of operations
// whatever else the Manager is holding.

/// Is a coin staged for an offer? (Single global slot — relaxation R3''.)
export ledger escrowActive: Boolean;
/// The staged coin, written with `Cell.writeCoin` so the ledger qualifies it with its Merkle index.
export ledger escrowCoin: QualifiedShieldedCoinInfo;
/// Whose staged coin it is — the only account allowed to open the swap, and the account already debited.
export ledger escrowOwner: Bytes<32>;

/// Is a received coin awaiting consolidation?
export ledger receivedActive: Boolean;
/// The coin the taker funded, parked here so the offer circuit never touches `pools`.
export ledger receivedCoin: QualifiedShieldedCoinInfo;
/// Who the received coin will be credited to when `consolidate` runs.
export ledger receivedOwner: Bytes<32>;

witness localOwnerSecret(): Bytes<32>;

circuit ownerCommitment(sk: Bytes<32>): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentCommit<Bytes<21>>("aa00005:manager:owner", disclose(sk));
}

circuit authenticatedAccount(): Bytes<32> {
  const acct = ownerCommitment(localOwnerSecret());
  assert(accounts.member(acct), "caller's owner witness matches no registered account");
  return acct;
}

circuit shieldedFamilyTag(): Bytes<32> {
  return pad(32, "aa00005:manager:shielded");
}

export circuit shieldedKey(acct: Bytes<32>, colour: Bytes<32>): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentHash<Vector<3, Bytes<32>>>([acct, colour, shieldedFamilyTag()]);
}

circuit shieldedBalanceOf(acct: Bytes<32>, colour: Bytes<32>): Uint<128> {
  const k = shieldedKey(acct, colour);
  return shieldedBalances.member(k) ? shieldedBalances.lookup(k) : 0;
}

export circuit registerAccount(owner: Bytes<32>): [] {
  const acct = disclose(owner);
  assert(!accounts.member(acct), "account already registered");
  accounts.insert(acct);
}

export circuit myAccount(): Bytes<32> {
  return ownerCommitment(localOwnerSecret());
}

export circuit isRegistered(owner: Bytes<32>): Boolean {
  return accounts.member(disclose(owner));
}

export circuit shieldedAccountBalance(owner: Bytes<32>, colour: Bytes<32>): Uint<128> {
  return shieldedBalanceOf(disclose(owner), disclose(colour));
}

export circuit poolValue(colour: Bytes<32>): Uint<128> {
  const col = disclose(colour);
  return pools.member(col) ? pools.lookup(col).value : 0;
}

export circuit poolHasColour(colour: Bytes<32>): Boolean {
  return pools.member(disclose(colour));
}

export circuit depositShielded(coin: ShieldedCoinInfo, account: Bytes<32>): [] {
  const c = disclose(coin);
  const acct = disclose(account);

  assert(c.value > 0, "deposit must be positive");
  assert(accounts.member(acct), "credit account is not registered");

  receiveShielded(c);

  const self = right<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>(kernel.self());
  if (pools.member(c.color)) {
    pools.insertCoin(c.color, mergeCoinImmediate(pools.lookup(c.color), c), self);
  } else {
    pools.insertCoin(c.color, c, self);
  }

  shieldedBalances.insert(
    shieldedKey(acct, c.color),
    (shieldedBalanceOf(acct, c.color) + c.value) as Uint<128>
  );
}

export circuit withdrawShielded(
  colour: Bytes<32>,
  value: Uint<128>,
  recipient: Either<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>
): ShieldedCoinInfo {
  const col = disclose(colour);
  const val = disclose(value);

  const acct = authenticatedAccount();
  assert(shieldedBalanceOf(acct, col) >= val, "account colour balance too low");
  assert(pools.member(col), "no pooled coin for this colour");
  assert(pools.lookup(col).value >= val, "pooled colour balance too low");

  const result = sendShielded(pools.lookup(col), disclose(recipient), val);

  if (result.change.is_some) {
    pools.insertCoin(col, result.change.value, right<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>(kernel.self()));
  } else {
    pools.remove(col);
  }

  shieldedBalances.insert(shieldedKey(acct, col), (shieldedBalanceOf(acct, col) - val) as Uint<128>);
  return result.sent;
}

export circuit transferInternalShielded(to: Bytes<32>, colour: Bytes<32>, amount: Uint<128>): [] {
  const dst = disclose(to);
  const col = disclose(colour);
  const amt = disclose(amount);

  const fromAcct = authenticatedAccount();
  assert(accounts.member(dst), "destination account is not registered");
  assert(fromAcct != dst, "internal transfer to the same account");
  assert(amt > 0, "internal transfer must be positive");
  assert(shieldedBalanceOf(fromAcct, col) >= amt, "account colour balance too low");

  shieldedBalances.insert(shieldedKey(fromAcct, col), (shieldedBalanceOf(fromAcct, col) - amt) as Uint<128>);
  shieldedBalances.insert(shieldedKey(dst, col), (shieldedBalanceOf(dst, col) + amt) as Uint<128>);
}

// --- zswap primitives the stdlib keeps private, as in v4 --------------------------------------

struct SwapCoinPreimage {
  domain_sep: Bytes<21>,
  info: ShieldedCoinInfo,
  dataType: Boolean,
  data: Bytes<32>,
}

circuit zswapCoinCommitment(
  coin: ShieldedCoinInfo,
  recipient: Either<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>
): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentHash<SwapCoinPreimage>(
    SwapCoinPreimage {
      domain_sep: "midnight:zswap-cc[v1]",
      info: coin,
      dataType: recipient.is_left,
      data: recipient.is_left ? recipient.left.bytes : recipient.right.bytes,
    });
}

circuit zswapCoinNullifier(coin: ShieldedCoinInfo, addr: ContractAddress): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentHash<SwapCoinPreimage>(
    SwapCoinPreimage {
      domain_sep: "midnight:zswap-cn[v1]",
      info: coin,
      dataType: false,
      data: addr.bytes,
    });
}

circuit dropMerkleIndex(coin: QualifiedShieldedCoinInfo): ShieldedCoinInfo {
  return ShieldedCoinInfo{ nonce: coin.nonce, color: coin.color, value: coin.value };
}

export circuit zswapNullifierOf(coin: ShieldedCoinInfo, addr: ContractAddress): Bytes<32> {
  return zswapCoinNullifier(coin, addr);
}

export circuit zswapCommitmentOf(
  coin: ShieldedCoinInfo,
  recipient: Either<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>
): Bytes<32> {
  return zswapCoinCommitment(coin, recipient);
}

// --- PHASE 1: stage. Self-balanced, map-heavy, NOT an offer. ----------------------------------

/// Move `value` of `colour` out of custody into the escrow cell, debiting the witness owner.
///
/// The full v4 guard order is kept, because this circuit is where the authorization actually happens:
/// the witness choke point, then the per-(account, colour) guard BEFORE the pool guard, with a missing
/// cell read as 0. The transaction it produces is internally balanced (`sendShielded` to this contract
/// creates an output the same call's input funds), so the maker submits it alone and no segment-0
/// placement question arises.
export circuit stageOffer(colour: Bytes<32>, value: Uint<128>): [] {
  const col = disclose(colour);
  const val = disclose(value);

  assert(val > 0, "stage must be positive");
  assert(!escrowActive.read(), "an offer is already staged");

  const acct = authenticatedAccount();
  assert(shieldedBalanceOf(acct, col) >= val, "account colour balance too low");
  assert(pools.member(col), "no pooled coin for this colour");
  const pooled = pools.lookup(col);
  assert(pooled.value >= val, "pooled colour balance too low");

  const selfRecipient = right<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>(kernel.self());
  // Sending to THIS contract auto-receives (stdlib `sendShielded`), so `sent` is a contract-owned coin
  // of exactly `val`, and its commitment is allocated in this transaction — which is what `writeCoin`
  // needs in order to qualify it with a Merkle index.
  const result = sendShielded(pooled, selfRecipient, val);

  if (result.change.is_some) {
    pools.insertCoin(col, result.change.value, selfRecipient);
  } else {
    pools.remove(col);
  }
  shieldedBalances.insert(shieldedKey(acct, col), (shieldedBalanceOf(acct, col) - val) as Uint<128>);

  escrowCoin.writeCoin(result.sent, selfRecipient);
  escrowOwner.write(acct);
  escrowActive.write(true);
}

// --- PHASE 2: the OFFER. Cells only. ---------------------------------------------------------

/// Give the WHOLE staged coin and require `coinB`, touching no custody map.
///
/// `recipientA` selects the FR-308 shape exactly as v4's `openSwapShielded` does:
///   some(key)  `sendShielded` pays the staged coin to that key; the staged value equals the payout so
///              there is no change, and the A leg is internally balanced — the offer's only imbalance
///              is the −B deficit (v1 named taker / v2b bearer key).
///   none       the staged coin is consumed as a zswap input with its nullifier claimed and NO output
///              created, so its value stands as a POSITIVE imbalance at the guaranteed segment,
///              addressed to nobody (v2a, the OPEN offer — owner-REQUIRED).
export circuit openSwap(
  recipientA: Maybe<Either<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>>,
  coinB: ShieldedCoinInfo
): [] {
  const rcpt = disclose(recipientA);
  const cb = disclose(coinB);

  // 0. parameter sanity + escrow state — all cells.
  assert(cb.value > 0, "swap must want a positive amount");
  assert(escrowActive.read(), "no offer is staged");
  assert(!receivedActive.read(), "a received coin is awaiting consolidation");
  const staged = escrowCoin.read();
  // F-305 made explicit, as in v4: two same-colour shielded credits cannot be assembled in one
  // transaction, so a same-colour swap is refused here with a readable reason.
  assert(staged.color != cb.color, "swap legs must be different colours");

  // 1. THE WITNESS CHOKE POINT — retained on purpose (see the header). This is the ONLY map read in
  //    this circuit, and it is the floor authorization imposes on the design.
  const acct = authenticatedAccount();
  assert(acct == escrowOwner.read(), "only the staging account may open the swap");

  const selfAddr = kernel.self();
  const selfRecipient = right<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>(selfAddr);

  if (rcpt.is_some) {
    // The whole staged coin goes out, so `change` is none and nothing is re-pooled.
    sendShielded(staged, rcpt.value, staged.value);
  } else {
    createZswapInput(staged);
    kernel.claimZswapNullifier(zswapCoinNullifier(dropMerkleIndex(staged), selfAddr));
  }

  // --- WANT: +B, parked in a CELL. No `pools`, no `shieldedBalances`, no hashing of a cell key. ---
  receiveShielded(cb);
  receivedCoin.writeCoin(cb, selfRecipient);
  receivedOwner.write(acct);
  receivedActive.write(true);

  escrowActive.write(false);
  escrowCoin.resetToDefault();
}

// --- PHASE 3: consolidate. Self-balanced, map-heavy, NOT an offer. ---------------------------

/// Merge the received coin into ordinary custody and credit `receivedOwner`.
///
/// The coin's commitment was allocated in the SETTLEMENT transaction, not this one, so it is already
/// qualified and must be placed with a plain `insert` (or merged with `mergeCoin`, which takes two
/// already-qualified coins). `insertCoin` would be wrong here: it re-derives a Merkle index from the
/// CURRENT transaction and this coin's index is not in it.
export circuit consolidate(): [] {
  assert(receivedActive.read(), "no received coin to consolidate");

  // Anyone holding a registered account may push the bookkeeping forward, but the credit always goes
  // to `receivedOwner` — recorded at settlement time and not a parameter — so this is a liveness
  // helper and not a way to redirect value.
  const caller = authenticatedAccount();
  assert(accounts.member(caller), "caller's owner witness matches no registered account");

  const coin = receivedCoin.read();
  const owner = receivedOwner.read();
  const selfRecipient = right<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>(kernel.self());

  if (pools.member(coin.color)) {
    pools.insertCoin(coin.color, mergeCoin(pools.lookup(coin.color), coin), selfRecipient);
  } else {
    pools.insert(coin.color, coin);
  }
  shieldedBalances.insert(
    shieldedKey(owner, coin.color),
    (shieldedBalanceOf(owner, coin.color) + coin.value) as Uint<128>
  );

  receivedActive.write(false);
  receivedCoin.resetToDefault();
}
